## Continuous random variables

Continuous random variables are conceptually more difficult to grasp. While discrete random variables have either finite or infinite sets of events but are still countable in some way, continuous random variables have an infinite number of events, usually from the set of real numbers $\mathbb{R}$ or from a subset of it. Such sets are not countably infinite anymore. Also, a quite counterintuitive characteristic of continuous random variables is that the probability linked to any event that is a single number (and not a range of values) has probability of zero due to the infinite amount of outcomes. This feature is derived from the *probability density function* (PDF) of a continuous random variable, which can be thought of as the equivalent of the PMF of a discrete random variable. However, PDFs behave quite differently.

A common and intuitive example is as follows. Assume you are going to a bus stop, and for some reason you do not have internet access and there is also no bus schedule at the bus stop. The only information that you have is that a bus from the bus line you would like to take stops there every fifteen minutes. So there are fifteen minutes of waiting time in between two buses, and since you have no further information on the arrival times, you could have come to the bus stop at any random time point in this fifteen minute interval. So at the exact time of your own arrival at the bus stop, the next bus could arrive at any time point during the next fifteen minutes, and the arrival is equally likely at each moment in that interval from your perspective. Let $Z$ be the random variable describing the time (in minutes) until the arrival of the next bus. Then its PDF $f_Z(z)$ is given by \begin{equation}
f_Z(z) = \begin{cases}
0,& \hspace{4mm} \text{ for } z \in T:= (-\infty, 0) \cup (15, \infty),\\
\frac{1}{15},&  \hspace{4mm} \text{ for } z \in [0, 15],
\end{cases}
\end{equation} which is depicted in the following figure.



::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Figure 3: An illustration of the PDF $f_Z(z)$. Black circles are included on the respective horizontal line, white ones are excluded.](index_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::



$f_Z(z)$ now does not provide $\text{Pr}(Z=z)$, because $\text{Pr}(Z=z)=0$ for all $z\in \mathbb{R}$ due to the continuous nature of the random variable. For a PDF, the area underneath the curve provides information about probabilities of ranges that $Z$ could fall into. The area underneath the entire curve is exactly 1, an important characteristic of PDFs, that can be shown easily for this example. Let $(v, w)\in \mathbb{R}^2$ be points in the previous plot with two axes. The area underneath $f_Z(z)$ is zero for both $z\in(-\infty, 0)$ and $z\in(15, \infty)$. The remaining area underneath the curve is enclosed by the rectangle defined through the four corners $(0,0)$, $(0,\frac{1}{15})$, $(15, \frac{1}{15})$ and $(15, 0)$. This square has width 15 (for $z$ ranging from 0 to 15) and height $\frac{1}{15}$ (for $f_Z(z)$ being equal to zero for $z\in T$ and $\frac{1}{15}$ otherwise). By common rule for the area of rectangles, the area in the defined rectangle must thus be $A=15 \times \frac{1}{15}=1$. As a further characteristic, PDFs always take on values $\geq 0$ and thus cannot become negative. To assess probabilities of ranges of $Z$, the idea of integration is usually required in more complex scenarios. Here, assume two different constants $a,b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a \leq b$. The area underneath $f_Z(z)$ for $a \leq z \leq b$ is equal to $\text{Pr}(a \leq Z \leq b)$, i.e. the probability that $Z$ will have a value between $a$ and $b$. The edge case is $a=b$, for which we then have $\text{Pr}(a \leq Z \leq b) = \text{Pr}(Z = a)=0$, as the area under the curve for a single point is zero.

The CDF in context of a continuous random variable is the same as for the discrete cases. However, due to the continuous nature, the CDF is an integral in the conintinuous setting. For our example, we have $F_Z(z) = \text{Pr}(Z\leq z)=\int_{-\infty}^{z}f_Z(x)\text{d}x$. For $z\in(-\infty,0)$, we must have $F_Z(z)=0$, as the area underneath $f_Z(z)$ is just zero there. Then for $z\in[0, 15]$, we can obtain \begin{align}
F_Z(z) &= \int_{0}^{z} \frac{1}{15}\text{d}x\\
&= \left[\frac{1}{15}x\right]_{0}^{z}\\
&= \frac{1}{15}z - \frac{1}{15}\times 0\\
&= \frac{1}{15}z.
\end{align} At $z=15$, we will have $F_Z(15)=1$. Since the area underneath $f_Z(z)$ is again zero for $z>15$, so that there is no further area to add, we will have $F_Z(z)=1$ for all $z\geq 15$. Therefore, for our example we have \begin{equation}
F_Z(z) = \begin{cases}
0,& \hspace{4mm} \text{ for } z \in (-\infty, 0),\\
\frac{1}{15}z,& \hspace{4mm} \text{ for } z \in [0, 15],\\
1,& \hspace{4mm} \text{ for } z \in (15, \infty).
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

